; UN-QUARANTINED 2026-07-24: the missed-wakeup deadlock this program
; triggered (~1-in-20 local runs; hung CI's Test step) is ROOT-CAUSED AND
; FIXED — it was a store-buffering memory-ordering bug, not a logic bug.
; task_wait_done's store(task[5])-then-load(task[4]) and the trampoline's
; store(task[4])-then-load(task[5]) formed a Dekker pair under
; release/acquire, which does not order a store before a later load of a
; different address; on Apple Silicon clang's acquire = LDAPR (RCpc), which
; may complete before an earlier STLR drains, so BOTH sides could read stale
; values: waiter parks forever, completer wakes nobody. Fixed with seq_cst
; on both pairs (24-in-500 hangs -> 1-in-1000), plus a wake permit in
; march_sched_wake/march_sched_park_self closing the residual
; wake-while-still-RUNNING drop (-> 0-in-3000). Full detail: specs/todos.md
; "Scheduler fork-join". The quarantine alias is kept as a convenience for
; soak-testing (`dune build @test/task_burst_await_quarantined`).
(rule
 (alias task_burst_await_quarantined)
 (action (diff native/task_burst_await.expected native_task_burst_await.out)))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action (diff native/task_burst_await.expected native_task_burst_await.out)))

; ── Phase 7.2: Signal.watch deferred dispatch (compiled) ──────────────
; A watched USR2 delivered by a self-raise must run its handler exactly once,
; from the scheduler drain, on a normal stack.  Dispatch is deferred, so the
; handler line prints after main's body — that ordering is the only thing
; separating a deferred drain from a synchronous in-Signal.raise call, which is
; why this golden pins it exactly rather than order-free.
; (source_tree ../stdlib) makes the whole stdlib — including signal.march —
; resolvable inside the sandbox.
;
; MARCH_NUM_SCHEDULERS=1 IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT LEFTOVER TUNING.
; march_signal_drain() runs at the top of EVERY sched_loop iteration on EVERY
; scheduler thread, so at the default of 4 the three idle workers race main for
; the self-raise's pending flag and run the handler concurrently with main's
; own println.  Measured 8/200 mismatches at the default vs 0/200 at 1 on the
; same binary (load avg ~14-17).  Both failure shapes were seen: `caught usr2`
; ahead of `after raise`, and the two threads' writes tearing into one line
; (`after raisecaught usr2`).  Pinning to one scheduler makes the deferral
; ordering a real guarantee — nothing else can run while main's green thread
; does, and march_sched_run's final drain is what then runs the handler before
; exit.  See specs/progress/2026-08-21-signal-watch-output-ordering-flake.md.
(rule
 (targets native_signal_watch native_signal_watch.out)
 (deps (file %{exe:../bin/main.exe})
       (file ../runtime/march_runtime.c) (file ../runtime/march_runtime.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_scheduler.c) (file ../runtime/march_scheduler.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_heap.c) (file ../runtime/march_heap.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_gc.c) (file ../runtime/march_gc.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_message.c) (file ../runtime/march_message.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_deque.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_extras.c) (file ../runtime/march_ctx_escape.c) (file ../runtime/march_compress.c)
       (file ../runtime/base64.c) (file ../runtime/sha1.c)
       (file native/signal_watch.march)
       (source_tree ../stdlib))
 (action
  (progn
   (run %{exe:../bin/main.exe} --compile -o native_signal_watch
        native/signal_watch.march)
   (with-stdout-to native_signal_watch.out
        (system "MARCH_NUM_SCHEDULERS=1 ./native_signal_watch")))))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action (diff native/signal_watch.expected native_signal_watch.out)))

; ── ...and the same binary at the DEFAULT (multi) scheduler count ──────
; Pinning the golden above to one scheduler would otherwise drop every bit of
; coverage of the drain firing from a WORKER thread, which is the path the
; Stage B design actually relies on for a signal arriving while green threads
; are busy.  This rule keeps that path exercised but asserts only what >1
; scheduler genuinely promises: the handler ran, and the process exited 0.
; The greps are deliberately UNANCHORED — with 4 schedulers the handler's
; println races main's and the two writev(2) calls tear into a shared line
; often enough that `^caught usr2$` would merely relocate the flake here
; (the tear falls between writev's two iovecs, so each message's own bytes
; stay contiguous and a substring match survives it).  Do not "tighten" these
; to anchored or ordered matches; read the ORDERING HAZARD note at the top of
; native/signal_watch.march first.
(rule
 (targets native_signal_watch_multisched.out)
 (deps (file native_signal_watch))
