; ── Local monitor Down payload (failing witness) ────────────────────────
; The watcher must receive Down(ref, target, Crash(msg)|Killed), rather than
; a mailbox-depth counter or a dropped control value. The bounded case also
; proves that a drop_new user mailbox does not drop the control-plane Down,
; and that mailbox_size counts the queued Down exactly once. The late case
; proves a monitor registered after death receives the stored reason. The
; single-scheduler run makes the terminal-watcher ownership check deterministic:
; its green thread cannot reach PROC_DEAD between main's two kill calls.
(rule
 (targets native_actor_monitor_down_reason native_actor_monitor_down_reason.out)
 (deps (file %{exe:../bin/main.exe})
       (file ../runtime/march_runtime.c) (file ../runtime/march_runtime.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_scheduler.c) (file ../runtime/march_scheduler.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_heap.c) (file ../runtime/march_heap.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_gc.c) (file ../runtime/march_gc.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_message.c) (file ../runtime/march_message.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_deque.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_extras.c) (file ../runtime/march_ctx_escape.c) (file ../runtime/march_compress.c)
       (file ../runtime/base64.c) (file ../runtime/sha1.c)
       (source_tree ../stdlib)
       (file native/actor_monitor_down_reason.march))
 (action
  (progn
   (run %{exe:../bin/main.exe} --compile -o native_actor_monitor_down_reason
        native/actor_monitor_down_reason.march)
   (with-stdout-to native_actor_monitor_down_reason.out
        (system "MARCH_NUM_SCHEDULERS=1 ./native_actor_monitor_down_reason")))))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (deps (file native_actor_monitor_down_reason.out))
 (action
 (run bash -c
   "grep -Eq '^down ref=[0-9]+ target=crashed reason=Crash\\(bang\\)$' native_actor_monitor_down_reason.out && grep -Eq '^down target=nulled reason=Crash\\(embedded-nul-ok\\)$' native_actor_monitor_down_reason.out && grep -Eq '^bounded mailbox=1 down=Killed ref=[0-9]+$' native_actor_monitor_down_reason.out && grep -Eq '^late down ref=[0-9]+ target=killed reason=Killed$' native_actor_monitor_down_reason.out && grep -Eq '^terminal watcher mailbox=0$' native_actor_monitor_down_reason.out")))

; Registration/alive publication stress: the compiled runtime uses four
; scheduler OS threads by default. Re-running the supervised crash witness
; exercises child preparation/registration, terminal publication, and Down
; delivery under cross-scheduler scheduling pressure.
;
; This loop used to redirect stdout to /dev/null and assert ONLY the exit
; code, so a multi-scheduler run that exited 0 while printing silently wrong
; Down payloads passed.  That is precisely the blind spot PR #310 lived in
; (a wake with an empty mailbox killed a live actor: compiled only, >1
; scheduler thread only, ~4% of runs) -- an exit-code-only loop cannot see a
; dropped or wrong reason string.  Each iteration now captures stdout and
; applies the SAME five assertions as the single-scheduler rule above.  They
; are deliberately order-independent greps and not a diff against the golden:
; at four schedulers these five lines legitimately interleave in a different
; order than the pinned run emits them, so only the SET of lines is stable.
; On failure the message names the iteration and the pattern and dumps the
; offending output -- a bare non-zero exit from inside a 100-iteration loop
; is miserable to debug in CI.
;
; Known PRE-EXISTING flake here, not introduced by the stdout capture: main
; sends PrimeBounded *before* kill(killed) (actor_monitor_down_reason.march
; lines 156-157) while the handler samples mailbox_size(watcher) at entry, so
; at >1 scheduler the watcher can be dispatched before the Down is enqueued
; and the binary exits 1 with `panic: bounded Down or mailbox count
; mismatch`.  Measured on a loaded 14-core box at 7/12000 runs with the OLD
; exit-code-only loop and 1/2500 with this one -- the rate is a property of
; the fixture, and the old loop's `|| exit $?` tripped on it identically.
; Tracked in specs/todos/2026-08-20-actor-monitor-bounded-mailbox-race.md.
(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (deps (file native_actor_monitor_down_reason))
 (action
  (run bash -c
   "check() { printf '%s\\n' \"$out\" | grep -Eq \"$1\" || { \
        printf 'native_actor_monitor_down_reason: MARCH_NUM_SCHEDULERS=4 iteration %s: output does not match /%s/\\noffending output:\\n%s\\n' \"$i\" \"$1\" \"$out\" >&2; \
        exit 1; }; }; \
    i=0; \
    while [ $i -lt 100 ]; do \
      out=$(MARCH_NUM_SCHEDULERS=4 ./native_actor_monitor_down_reason); rc=$?; \
      [ $rc -eq 0 ] || { \
        printf 'native_actor_monitor_down_reason: MARCH_NUM_SCHEDULERS=4 iteration %s: binary exited %s\\noffending output:\\n%s\\n' \"$i\" \"$rc\" \"$out\" >&2; \
        exit 1; }; \
      check '^down ref=[0-9]+ target=crashed reason=Crash\\(bang\\)$'; \
      check '^down target=nulled reason=Crash\\(embedded-nul-ok\\)$'; \
      check '^bounded mailbox=1 down=Killed ref=[0-9]+$'; \
      check '^late down ref=[0-9]+ target=killed reason=Killed$'; \
      check '^terminal watcher mailbox=0$'; \
      i=$((i + 1)); \
    done")))

; ── Named registry (Task 5): registry_retire_actor on actor death ────────
; (compiled + interpreted parity) ─────────────────────────────────────
; What actor_registry_basic.march does NOT cover: a killed actor's name
; must be fully RECLAIMED, not just resolve to None at lookup time (that
; part is march_actor_whereis's own $alive re-check, present since Task 4,
; independent of retire). Registers "worker" to actor a, kills a, confirms
; the name drops out of Actor.registered(), then registers a DIFFERENT
; actor b under the same name and confirms it succeeds and resolves.
; Uses Actor, hence source_tree ../stdlib.
;
; CORRECTION (fix round 1, review finding): this fixture's assertions do
; NOT actually discriminate whether do_actor_death's retire call ran, on
; the COMPILED backend — march_actor_registered/whereis filter on the
; $alive flag regardless of the forward-table entry, and name reuse
; succeeds via Task 3's stale-overwrite branch regardless of retire too.
; Verified by temporarily deleting the C call: this fixture's output was
; unchanged. It DOES discriminate the interpreter's `crash_actor`, whose
; `named_registry` Hashtbl has no such $alive-style filter of its own —
; verified by temporarily reverting the `Hashtbl.remove` loop there and
; re-running this file interpreted (not dune-wired; see
; test/native/actor_registry_retire.march's usage note and the Task 5
; report for the before/after). The genuine COMPILED-side discriminator
; is the sibling fixture below, actor_registry_retire_vault.march, which
; reads the raw (non-liveness-filtered) registry table directly.
(rule
 (targets native_actor_registry_retire native_actor_registry_retire.out)
 (deps (file %{exe:../bin/main.exe})
       (file ../runtime/march_runtime.c) (file ../runtime/march_runtime.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_scheduler.c) (file ../runtime/march_scheduler.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_heap.c) (file ../runtime/march_heap.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_gc.c) (file ../runtime/march_gc.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_message.c) (file ../runtime/march_message.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_deque.h)
       (file ../runtime/march_extras.c) (file ../runtime/march_ctx_escape.c) (file ../runtime/march_compress.c)
       (file ../runtime/base64.c) (file ../runtime/sha1.c)
       (source_tree ../stdlib)
       (file native/actor_registry_retire.march))
 (action
  (progn
   (run %{exe:../bin/main.exe} --compile -o native_actor_registry_retire
        native/actor_registry_retire.march)
   (with-stdout-to native_actor_registry_retire.out
        (system "./native_actor_registry_retire")))))

